 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/De-Dyn-SPWG/",
 "title": "Two Small Changes to Accelerate Your Career",
 "topic": "mindset",
 "summary": "The creator explains two simple changes you can make to speed up your career growth. Instead of focusing on your hard work activity, report on the actual changes and impact you created.",
 "key_points": [
  "Start reporting on change rather than activity.",
  "People do not care how hard you worked; they only care about what changed because of your work.",
  "Structure headlines as: \"Things were this way, now they are this way.\"",
  "Early in your career, you grow by showcasing that you can get stuff done.",
  "Continued growth comes by showcasing how you think.",
  "Ask for forgiveness more than you ask for permission.",
  "Sending decisions up the chain creates work for others and stops you from showcasing your judgment.",
  "Be bolder about making the call and putting your opinions out in the open."
 ],
 "content": "Low-effort way to accelerate your career. Two small changes make in their career to speed up their career growth. The first thing, start reporting on change rather than activity. What I see with a lot of people, especially more junior employees, they want people to see how hard they worked. So they described the work, what they did, how long it took. The problem is that nobody cares how hard you worked. They only care about what changed because of your work. But when you report things to your boss you're only going to get a little bit of their attention. So the headline needs to be things were this way, now they're this way. Second small thing that if people would change they would grow in their career at a way faster rate. What you have to understand is that you're going to grow quickly early in your career by showcasing that you can get stuff done. Continued growth comes by showcasing how you think. And so the change is start asking for forgiveness a lot more than you ask for permission. Every time you send a decision up the chain to be made, first of all you're creating work for somebody, which they don't love. But more importantly, you're foregoing the opportunity to showcase your judgment in the event that you got it right. If you got it wrong, you're foregoing an opportunity to refine your judgment. Either way, be bolder about making the call on something, about putting your opinions out there in the open. I know it can be scary, especially for you more junior employees, but I promise good things will come of it. \n\n[Text on Screen]: Low-effort way to accelerate your career",
 "status": "done",
